1
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Where all the other Bonds end… this one begins!

2
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Moonraker is a action, adventure, thriller film directed by Lewis Gilbert.

3
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:06,000
After Drax Industries' Moonraker space shuttle is hijacked, secret agent James Bond is assigned to
investigate, traveling to California to meet the company's owner, the mysterious Hugo Drax.

4
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:46,000
With the help of scientist Dr.

5
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:26,000
Holly Goodhead, Bond soon uncovers Drax's nefarious plans for humanity, all the while fending off an
old nemesis, Jaws, and venturing to Venice, Rio, the Amazon...and even outer space.

6
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
James Bond, played by Roger Moore.

7
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:46,000
Dr. Holly Goodhead, played by Lois Chiles.

8
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:26,000
Hugo Drax, played by Michael Lonsdale.

9
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:06,000
Jaws, played by Richard Kiel.

10
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:46,000
Corinne Dufour, played by Corinne Cléry.

11
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:26,000
M, played by Bernard Lee.

12
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:06,000
Moonraker is a 1979 spy-fi film, the eleventh in the James Bond series produced by Eon
Productions, and the fourth to star Roger Moore as the fictional MI6 agent James Bond.

13
01:00:40,000 --> 01:00:46,000
The third and final film in the series to be directed by Lewis Gilbert, it
co-stars Lois Chiles, Michael Lonsdale, Corinne Cléry and Richard Kiel.

14
01:05:20,000 --> 01:05:26,000
In the film, Bond investigates the vanishing of a Space Shuttle orbiter, leading
him to Hugo Drax, the owner of the shuttle's manufacturing firm.

15
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
Along with astronaut Dr.

16
01:14:40,000 --> 01:14:46,000
Holly Goodhead, Bond follows the mystery from California to Venice, Rio de Janeiro, the Amazon rainforest, and finally into
outer space to prevent a plot to wipe out the world population and repopulate humanity with a master race.

17
01:19:20,000 --> 01:19:26,000
The story was intended by author Ian Fleming to become a film even before he completed the
novel in 1954; he based it on a screenplay manuscript he had devised earlier.

18
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
The film's producers had originally intended to make For Your Eyes Only, but chose Moonraker as a result of the resurgence of the
science fiction genre, following the success of Star Wars, and NASA's concurrent test flights of the Space Shuttle.

19
01:28:40,000 --> 01:28:46,000
Budgetary issues led to the film being shot primarily in France; other
locations included Italy, Brazil, Guatemala and the United States.

20
01:33:20,000 --> 01:33:26,000
The soundstages of Pinewood Studios in England, traditionally used for
the series, were only used by the special effects team.

21
01:38:00,000 --> 01:38:06,000
Moonraker had a high production cost of $34 million, more than twice as much
as The Spy Who Loved Me (1977), and it received mixed reviews.

22
01:42:40,000 --> 01:42:46,000
However, the film's visuals were praised, with Derek Meddings being nominated for the Academy Award for Best Visual Effects, and it eventually
became the highest-grossing film of the series at the time with $210.3 million worldwide, a record that stood until 1995's GoldenEye.

23
01:47:20,000 --> 01:47:26,000
This was Bernard Lee's final outing as M before his death in January 1981.

24
01:52:00,000 --> 01:52:06,000
The next film in the series, For Your Eyes Only, followed in 1981.

25
01:56:40,000 --> 01:56:46,000
The film explores themes of space marine, based on novel or
book, venice, italy, mass murder, rio de janeiro.

26
02:01:20,000 --> 02:01:26,000
The tone is thought_provoking and intense.
